<p>So, let=E2=80=99s think of another way to detect overflow.</p>
<p>We're constructing the number <code>reversed_n</code> one digit at a tim=
e, which means we need to ensure not to cause the number to overflow with e=
ach new digit we add. Let's think about when adding a new digit might cause=
 <code>reversed_n</code> to become too large. Here's how we can analyze thi=
s:</p>
<p>If <code>reversed_n</code> is equal to <code>214748364</code> (i.e., <co=
de>INT_MAX // 10</code>), then the final digit we can add to it must be les=
s than or equal to 7 to avoid an overflow (since <code>214748364**7** =3D=
=3D INT_MAX</code>):</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
