<p>This means that when <code>reversed_n =3D=3D INT_MAX // 10</code>, the l=
ast digit added to it won=E2=80=99t cause an overflow, meaning we don=E2=80=
=99t need to check the last digit in this case.</p>
<p>If <code>reversed_n</code> is already larger than <code>INT_MAX // 10</c=
ode>, adding any digit will cause it to overflow. We can handle this case w=
ith the following condition:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>if reversed_n &gt; INT_MAX // 10: return 0</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Detecting integer underflow</strong><br>
We can apply similar logic to the above for handling integer underflow. Her=
e, we just need to check that <code>reversed_n</code> never falls below <co=
de>INT_MIN // 10</code>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>if reversed_n &lt; INT_MIN // 10: return 0</code></p>
</blockquote>
